WHY THE AGPL, AND NOT THE GPL
-----------------------------

OBJEKAT itself would sit happily under the GPL. The Affero clause is inherited:
the app is built on Tracktion Engine, which cannot run without JUCE, and the
free option of JUCE 8 is the AGPLv3. Section 13 of the GPLv3 expressly allows a
GPLv3 work to be combined with an AGPLv3 one, and states that the AGPL's network
requirements then apply to the combination. The AGPLv3 is therefore the licence
that covers the whole.

In practice, for a desktop application that people run on their own machine,
that clause never comes into play: it is about users interacting with the
software remotely, over a network. The command API is a local UNIX socket, not
a network service.

Anyone holding commercial licences for Tracktion Engine and JUCE is not bound by
this reasoning, and may license their own work as they see fit.


